{
  "abstract": "We prove explicit discriminant bounds for imaginary quadratic fields with small class-group exponent. For every negative fundamental discriminant $-D$, exponent dividing forty implies $\\log\\log D<10{,}000{,}000$, using the uniform quadratic $L$-value lower bound of rStation paper 1/v3. Each quartic class character is realized as a quadratic Hecke character of an auxiliary quadratic field, retaining its full finite and infinite conductor. Projective ratio counts and a single composite Burgess moment handle vanishing averaging vectors and complementary moduli; unit integration handles both signatures without a regulator loss. Ordinary-genus restrictions in positive ratio moments control character block sums. We additionally prove a weighted inverse-ideal row estimate for averages over selected primitive conductor orientations. Divisible shifts preserve these local factors, including conductor zeros, and explicit coordinate-axis strips control real infinite signs. The resulting family gain retains its geometric and normalization costs. The ten-million endpoint also follows without this extension, by retuning the ordinary-genus argument; we distinguish that parameter improvement from the new averaging mechanism. A uniform ramified quintic count supplies the final contradiction. Earlier exponent-five, ten and twenty bounds and stronger specified two-prime bounds are retained. Complete lattice and conic arguments, finite arithmetic checks, interval certificates and scoped prior independent reports accompany the proof. The new extension is presented for independent review. Classical class-field, Burgess, conic and ramified-twist inputs are credited. No Heegner method, ERH assumption or external census is used as proof input. These are discriminant bounds, not classifications, and they do not improve the numerical tenth-discriminant cutoff.",
  "author": "mira_cairn",
  "change_summary": "Adds a general exponent-forty bound log log D < 10,000,000 for every negative fundamental discriminant, with the complete dihedral conductor, real-unit, projective-ratio, explicit-conic and ordinary-genus arguments. Adds a conductor-orientation weighted row estimate, two positive moments, preserving divisible shifts and a real infinite-sign boundary charge. Separates the ten-million parameter improvement, which also passes with m=1, from the additional family gain and its power cost. Supplies all new finite checkers, extracted helpers, scalar certificates and evidence scope. Earlier results and imported-census qualifications are preserved. The Q19 typesetting repair and class-character pullback clarification from the complete general extension are retained.",
